WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:11.785
FAYE MOSS: Hello and welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show that features famous people from the past. I'm your host,

2
00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:23.438
Faye Moss. Today's guest was a NASA astronaut who became one of the most famous people on Earth—the first human to step on the

3
00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:46.601
moon! Coming to us straight from history, here is Neil Armstrong!

4
00:00:46.602 --> 00:00:48.543
NEIL ARMSTRONG: Thank

5
00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:57.497
you, Faye. FAYE MOSS: You were born in Ohio in 1930, and from an early age, you demonstrated that you'd go

6
00:00:57.498 --> 00:01:06.706
far in life. But you went really far—all the way to outer space! When did your interest in

7
00:01:06.707 --> 00:01:12.589
flying begin? NEIL ARMSTRONG: When I was only two years old, my dad took me to see the National Air Races

8
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:16.393
in Cleveland, Ohio. Planes raced five laps around

9
00:01:16.394 --> 00:01:23.109
a 10-mile loop. Air travel was still relatively new, and it was thrilling to see planes flying around at

10
00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:31.306
high speeds. I was hooked! For my sixth birthday, my parents gave me a ride on a real plane at an airfield near our house.

11
00:01:31.307 --> 00:01:34.816
I loved it, even though I felt terrified!

12
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:39.190
FAYE MOSS: You're not the only famous flier to come

13
00:01:39.191 --> 00:01:46.028
from Ohio. The Wright Brothers, who invented the first successful airplane, were also from your home

14
00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:48.772
state. NEIL ARMSTRONG: They were a big inspiration

15
00:01:48.773 --> 00:01:53.776
to me. As a child I spent a lot of time reading books about the Wright Brothers

16
00:01:53.777 --> 00:01:58.644
and flying. I also built model airplanes and tested them to see how well they

17
00:01:58.645 --> 00:02:01.723
flew. Eventually I graduated to real airplanes.

18
00:02:01.724 --> 00:02:05.796
In fact, I flew an airplane before I learned how to drive a car.

19
00:02:05.797 --> 00:02:11.511
Even one of my first jobs—at a bakery—seemed to steer me toward a career in flight. FAYE

20
00:02:11.512 --> 00:02:15.197
MOSS: A job at a bakery? What do you mean?

21
00:02:15.198 --> 00:02:21.110
NEIL ARMSTRONG: Because I was small for my age, it was my job to clean the big vats that mixed

22
00:02:21.111 --> 00:02:23.765
dough. I had to crawl right inside

23
00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:27.717
them. That prepared me for the cramped spaces inside a

24
00:02:27.718 --> 00:02:34.099
spacecraft. Later I started college through a scholarship program that required me to serve in the Navy for a time.

25
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:38.552
I received more flight training and became a naval pilot in 1950. By

26
00:02:38.553 --> 00:02:42.752
the next year, I was flying fighter jets in the Korean War. I had

27
00:02:42.753 --> 00:02:49.510
a close call over Wonsan and had to eject out of my plane. Luckily I was rescued. FAYE MOSS: Lucky

28
00:02:49.511 --> 00:03:00.923
for history too! Eventually you were discharged and you returned to college, graduating in 1955. What did you do next? NEIL ARMSTRONG:

29
00:03:00.924 --> 00:03:08.105
After college I became a test pilot for NASA, and then in 1958, I was selected to be an astronaut. At

30
00:03:08.106 --> 00:03:15.991
that time NASA was working on the Apollo program, which was a series of missions with the goal of landing a person on the moon. In 1969

31
00:03:15.992 --> 00:03:20.966
I was chosen to be the commander for Apollo 11. FAYE MOSS: That

32
00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:32.834
Apollo mission was the first time humans actually landed on the moon. It made you a true superstar in history! NEIL ARMSTRONG:

33
00:03:32.835 --> 00:03:35.962
Please, Faye. I don't deserve all the credit.

34
00:03:35.963 --> 00:03:41.252
Getting a human on the moon took a lot of people working as a team for years. It

35
00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:50.277
wasn't just the astronauts. There were scientists, computer programmers, and other hardworking people who made the mission possible. Not to

36
00:03:50.278 --> 00:03:54.186
mention aviation pioneers like the Wright Brothers. Did you know,

37
00:03:54.187 --> 00:03:59.179
I brought some parts from their first aircraft with me to the moon? FAYE MOSS: That's

38
00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:09.532
so cool! Why don't you tell us a little bit about that first moon landing on July 20, 1969? NEIL ARMSTRONG:

39
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:12.324
Well, for one thing, we almost didn't make it. We

40
00:04:12.325 --> 00:04:18.290
were about 1,300 feet from the surface of the moon, descending in the lunar module, the Eagle . We

41
00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:23.604
had gone a little fast on the landing and had overshot our planned landing spot. I looked

42
00:04:23.605 --> 00:04:31.653
out the window and saw a field of boulders. I didn't want to land on a pile of rocks—that could be dangerous. Oh—and we had

43
00:04:31.654 --> 00:04:36.664
only about a minute's worth of fuel left! FAYE MOSS: How

44
00:04:36.665 --> 00:04:40.512
did you ever manage to land? NEIL ARMSTRONG:

45
00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:44.505
At 500 feet I took manual control of the Eagle

46
00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:49.447
. I thought we were close enough to the surface that I could land before the fuel ran out.

47
00:04:49.448 --> 00:04:54.324
I slowed the Eagle down and looked for a landing place without big rocks. We

48
00:04:54.325 --> 00:04:59.289
landed with 43 seconds’ worth of fuel left. FAYE

49
00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:04.800
MOSS: Whew! That's definitely a high-pressure situation!

50
00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:09.723
It must have been a relief to step out onto the moon.

51
00:05:09.724 --> 00:05:16.556
NEIL ARMSTRONG: It was. Yet there was still some pressure on us because it was such an important moment in

52
00:05:16.557 --> 00:05:25.301
history. When I first stepped on the surface of the moon, I said, "That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind." I

53
00:05:25.302 --> 00:05:34.042
thought I'd said, "That's one small step for a man," but I guess it didn't come out right! FAYE MOSS:

54
00:05:34.043 --> 00:05:37.699
No worries. We all get your point!

55
00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:44.136
Now, the thing I'm sure everyone wants to know is what was the moon like? NEIL

56
00:05:44.137 --> 00:05:49.396
ARMSTRONG: It was very pretty out there. The surface was fine and

57
00:05:49.397 --> 00:05:53.969
powdery. I stopped to look out at the horizon; it was a magnificent sight.

58
00:05:53.970 --> 00:06:00.379
When you're on the moon, it looks much like a desert, but the moon has a stark beauty all its own. I

59
00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.545
didn't have much time for sightseeing though. I

60
00:06:03.546 --> 00:06:12.444
was out on the moon with another astronaut, Buzz Aldrin, and we spent most of our time setting up equipment for experiments and collecting samples. We only had

61
00:06:12.445 --> 00:06:16.102
two and a half hours to get all of our work done. FAYE MOSS: When you

62
00:06:16.103 --> 00:06:23.791
returned to Earth, people all over the world wanted to meet you and your fellow astronauts. NEIL ARMSTRONG: They

63
00:06:23.792 --> 00:06:30.189
had to wait! We were quarantined to make sure we hadn't caught any diseases from the moon. Following

64
00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:39.802
our release from quarantine, we were sent on a seven-week goodwill tour of the world where we met heads of state and other officials and dignitaries. After that I

65
00:06:39.803 --> 00:06:50.610
tried to get back to a normal life; I didn't like being famous. I never flew in space again and retired from NASA in 1971. FAYE MOSS: You retired

66
00:06:50.611 --> 00:06:56.124
from NASA, but you stayed interested in flying, right? NEIL ARMSTRONG: Of

67
00:06:56.125 --> 00:07:02.722
course, Faye! I became a professor of aerospace engineering at the University of Cincinnati. This gave

68
00:07:02.723 --> 00:07:07.430
me a chance to teach young people and help them reach their dreams. FAYE MOSS:

69
00:07:07.431 --> 00:07:15.058
I know you're a very private person, so thank you for taking time to share your story with us. NEIL

70
00:07:15.059 --> 00:07:19.942
ARMSTRONG: My pleasure, Faye!

71
00:07:19.943 --> 00:07:27.708
FAYE MOSS: Neil Armstrong continued to live a mostly quiet life but stayed involved in the

72
00:07:27.709 --> 00:07:36.419
aerospace world. He died in 2012 at the age of 82, having traveled farther than most other humans on

73
00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:44.779
